---
title: "The database"
description: "Schema, migrations, the four SQL rules that are non-negotiable, and the tier-minutes CTE with its explicit type oids."
---

Postgres 17. Four tables, three migrations, and all the SQL in the project lives
under `src/db/`. No ORM, no query builder: postgres.js tagged templates, which
parameterise by construction.

## The schema

```{mermaid}
%%| label: fig-schema
%%| fig-cap: "Four tables. days.tier is the only ground truth about activity."
erDiagram
  guilds ||--o{ users : "guild_slug"
  guilds ||--o{ chats : "guild_slug"
  users  ||--o{ days  : "telegram_id"

  guilds {
    text    slug PK
    text    name
    integer member_count
  }
  users {
    bigint      telegram_id PK
    text        guild_slug FK
    text        first_name
    text        username
    smallint    reminder_hour
    boolean     reminder_asked
    smallint    ignored_streak
    boolean     blocked
    timestamptz last_reminded_at
    timestamptz joined_at
  }
  days {
    bigint      telegram_id PK
    date        date PK
    text        tier
    text        tag
    timestamptz logged_at
  }
  chats {
    bigint      chat_id PK
    text        guild_slug FK
    bigint      pinned_message_id
    text        pinned_text
    boolean     pin_failed
    date        last_monday_week
    timestamptz bound_at
  }
```

Things worth noticing:

- **`days` has a composite primary key**, `(telegram_id, date)`. One row per
  person per day is enforced by the key rather than by any duplicate-detection
  logic, so FR-7's "logging twice replaces" is an `ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE`.
- **`days.tag` exists and nothing writes it.** FR-11's optional tag was **cut**,
  not deferred (`SPEC.md` §9 Q5). The column was declared in migration 001 from
  `SPEC.md` §6 and left alone. Do not read it as pending work.
- **`chats` is a table, not a column on `guilds`.** `SPEC.md` §6 offered both.
  Pin state and the Monday ledger are facts about a chat, one guild plausibly has
  both a board chat and a general chat, and unbinding must not touch a `guilds`
  row that `syncGuilds` rewrites on every boot.
- **`chats.last_monday_week` is `NOT NULL`.** `NULL` would read as "owed a post"
  and fire a "new week, back to zero" message at a chat bound on a Thursday.

## Migrations

`src/db/migrate.ts` is 36 lines. Numbered `.sql` files in `src/db/migrations/`,
each applied once inside its own transaction, recorded by filename in a
`migrations` table. Re-running is a no-op, so a restart is free (NFR-5).

There is no down migration and no rollback. To change the schema, add
`004_<name>.sql`. Migration 003 is worth reading as a model: it does an `ALTER
TABLE` and then a careful data migration whose comment explains why it marks only
*some* rows as asked, because marking everyone would silently convert "never
asked" into "declined" for exactly the users the ambiguity applied to.

## The four SQL rules

These are not style. Each one has a failure mode that is silent.

### 1. Every `BIGINT` and `DATE` is selected `::text`

postgres.js returns `BIGINT` as a JavaScript string and `DATE` as a `Date` object
at UTC midnight. Telegram IDs are `BIGINT`, supergroup chat IDs are large and
negative, and every day bucket is a `DATE`.

Selecting either raw is exactly where an ID stops matching the one Telegram sent,
or a date shifts by a day under formatting. The column lists in `src/db/users.ts`
and `src/db/chats.ts` do this centrally; new queries must do it themselves.

### 2. Every per-member division casts `::numeric` before dividing, then `::float8`

```sql
(COALESCE(SUM(t.minutes), 0)::numeric / g.member_count)::float8 AS "perMember"
```

Postgres integer division truncates `142 / 650` to **0**. Without the first cast,
every guild's per-member score is zero until it passes a full member-count of
minutes. The `::float8` afterwards is so the driver returns a number rather than a
numeric string.

::: {.callout-warning}
## The query printed in `SPEC.md` §6 has this bug

`SPEC.md` §6 prints an illustrative standings query that truncates its own
division. It is a sketch, not a reference implementation. Do not copy it, and do
not "restore" the working query to match it.
:::

### 3. Every ranking `ORDER BY` needs a unique tiebreaker

Guild names are not unique in the schema (only slugs are the primary key), and
first names are certainly not: two people called Alex who have both logged
nothing are ordinary in a 650-member guild.

Without a unique tiebreaker, Postgres may return different orderings across
successive calls, so the same user could see different neighbours on two
consecutive `/me` invocations. Every ordered query in the project therefore ends
with `g.slug` or `telegram_id`.

Under a `LIMIT` this does more than stabilise output. `dueReminders()` is capped at
25 per tick, and without the tiebreaker Postgres may return a different page each
call, so a user could be **starved** rather than merely reordered.

### 4. Date buckets are computed in SQL, in `Europe/Helsinki`, never in JavaScript

`src/db/calendar.ts` is where today, yesterday, the current week's Monday and the
local hour all come from:

```sql
WITH local AS (SELECT (COALESCE($1::timestamptz, now()) AT TIME ZONE 'Europe/Helsinki') AS ts)
SELECT (ts)::date::text                          AS today,
       (ts::date - INTERVAL '1 day')::date::text AS yesterday,
       date_trunc('week', ts)::date::text        AS week_start,
       EXTRACT(HOUR FROM ts)::int                AS hour
FROM local
```

The competition window straddles a clock change, and Postgres has a real timezone
database where `now() + 7 * 24 hours` does not. `date_trunc('week', ...)` is
already ISO Monday-based, which is what the weekly reset needs.

**The one deliberate exception** is `previousWeek()` in `domain/scoring.ts` and its
sibling `dayBefore()` in `domain/scheduling.ts`. Both do UTC-midnight label
arithmetic with **no timezone conversion at all**: the input and output are both
`yyyy-mm-dd` calendar labels, so minus seven days is exact regardless of what
Helsinki's offset is on either date. The rule exists to stop a timezone conversion
drifting a boundary, and there is no conversion in them. Each carries a comment
saying so.

The `at` parameter on `calendar()`, `dueReminders()`, `setReminderHour()` and
`resumeReminders()` pins a fixed instant **for tests only**. Production callers
pass nothing and get `now()`.

## The tier-minutes CTE

`src/db/tiers.ts` is 51 lines, of which 40 are a comment. It builds this:

```sql
SELECT * FROM unnest($1::text[], $2::int[])
```

injected as a CTE into the four queries that convert tiers into minutes. It is
built from `config.ts` on every call and is **never a table**, which is what makes
a tier-value edit recompute all history.

::: {.callout-important}
## The explicit oids are load-bearing

```ts
sql.array(TIER_NAMES, 1009)   // text[]
sql.array(TIER_VALUES, 1007)  // int4[]
```

postgres.js resolves an array parameter's wire type at query **construction**
time, from a per-client cache that is only populated after that client has
completed one prior round trip. A standings or days query issued as the very
first query on a fresh client would otherwise serialise both arrays as bare,
brace-less comma-joined text, which Postgres rejects as a malformed array
literal. The `::text[]` and `::int[]` casts do not help: the fault is in the wire
parameter, not the SQL.

Before this fix, production was safe only incidentally, because `main.ts` calls
`waitForDatabase()` (a `SELECT 1`) first and that round trip happened to warm the
cache. A maintainer moving to a managed always-on database could reasonably
delete that call, which would arm the bug with no signal.

`tests/db/tiers.test.ts` pins it against a genuinely cold client and fails
without the oids.
:::

## The queries in `standings.ts`

Four of them, and one thing is conspicuously absent from three.

| Function | Returns |
|---|---|
| `standings(from, to)` | Every guild's total and per-member score for a date range, best first |
| `neighbours(id, guild, from, to)` | The user and at most one person either side, inside their guild |
| `weeklyTotals(id)` | One row per week the user has any record in, feeding the streak |
| `participation(guild, from, to)` | The share of a guild's roster that logged at least once |

**There is no `NOT u.blocked` in any of them, and adding one is a defect.** The
clause appears in the query printed in `SPEC.md` §6, and it was removed from all
three sites together. The reasoning is in [Invariants](../invariants.tmd); the
short version is that `blocked` gates outbound messaging and never scoring, and
because no total is stored, the clause is retroactive: the first user who blocks
the bot would erase their whole season from their guild's total, and the pinned
message would publish the drop.

Three tests in `tests/db/standings.test.ts` guard this, one per query. They pass
trivially today and only earn their keep the day someone re-adds the clause.

`participation()` deliberately does **not** join `tier_minutes` at all: a rest day
counts, because it measures engagement rather than minutes.

## The guarded undo

`undoDay()` in `src/db/days.ts` is the subtlest write in the project. It takes both
the tier to restore and the tier it **expects** to find, and matches on the latter
in the `WHERE` clause of both statements.

The reason is that a confirmation message stays in the chat with a live Undo
button indefinitely (NFR-5), so two confirmations for the same day can be live at
once: one from the daily reminder, one from a later `/log` that corrected the
tier. Applying the older payload unconditionally reverted the newer entry, and
deleted the day outright when its `restore` was null, while the toast said "Put
back".

Two further details:

- The check is **in the SQL** rather than a read followed by a write, so there is
  no window between them for a concurrent tap.
- The restore path is an `UPDATE`, not the upsert it used to be. `ON CONFLICT`
  makes a missing row an insert rather than a no-op, which would resurrect a day
  somebody had already undone from another message.

The function returns whether it applied, and the handler shows `UNDO_SUPERSEDED`
when it did not, so the bot never reports a change it did not make.
